# Archive folders — tracked in git for history, never imported by production code
# SvelteKit only bundles what's imported — _archive/ files are safe to commit
# Verify with: grep -r "/_archive/" src/routes/ src/lib/ --include="*.svelte" --include="*.ts" -l | grep -v _archive/

# Platform specific (Capacitor iOS build dir at root — never the bare `ios`
# pattern which matches at any depth, including legitimate iOS-related
# directories that may exist deeper in the tree)
/ios/
.kiro

# Lock files (using pnpm)
package-lock.json
yarn.lock

# Static generated
static/meta.json

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GITIGNORE DEPTH NOTES — read before editing patterns above
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A pattern with NO leading slash matches at EVERY depth in the repo. So
# `coverage` matches both /coverage/ and src/foo/coverage/. This is the
# default Git behavior and it is a foot-gun for short common names.
#
# Use `/<name>` or `/<name>/` to restrict a pattern to the repo ROOT only.
# Use `<name>` (no slashes) only when you genuinely want at-any-depth match
# (node_modules, .DS_Store, *.log, editor swap files like *~ / *.swp).
#
# When in doubt, prefer leading-slash. If you've ever had `git add` silently
# refuse a file, run `git check-ignore -v <path>` — it tells you exactly
# which line in this file rejected it.
#
# History: the bare `coverage` pattern silently hid the QA Coverage Map
# route at src/routes/dashboard/admin/qa/coverage/ for 6 days (Phase 5 of
# S78 — files on disk, never tracked, no warning). Don't repeat the trap.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
